import — Read a data file into a frame                                       data · tier 1 · since 0.1.0 · 1.0 · exact

IN BRIEF
    - `import("data/wages.csv")`: a file of the project, the type of each column guessed from its first
      1,000 rows.
    - `import("data/wages.csv", types: {id: Str})`: a type declared where the guess is not the one wanted.
    - `import("data/sales.csv", decimal: ",", thousands: ".")`: a file written with a decimal comma.
    - `import("lfs:wages.csv")`: a file of a data root declared in `zeff.toml`.
    - `import("zeff:wages.csv")`: a file that ships with Zeff.

USAGE
    import(path, delim: Str, header: Bool = true, skip: Int = 0, na_values: List, types: Record,
           names: keep | snake = keep, quote: Str, decimal: Str, thousands: Str, encoding: Str = "utf-8")

DESCRIPTION
    Reads a file in the project (`import("data/wages.csv")`) or in a declared data root
    (`import("lfs:wages.parquet")`) and chooses the reader from the extension. This release reads delimited
    text: CSV, TSV and text files, following RFC 4180, with the delimiter detected from the first line when
    it is `;` or a tab, and with every option of a delimited file: another quote character or none
    (`quote:`), a decimal comma (`decimal: ","`, which makes `;` the usual delimiter), grouped digits
    (`thousands: "."`), and a file that is not UTF-8 (`encoding: "windows-1252"`, any name of the WHATWG
    list). The other formats of section 6.1 arrive with release 0.11.0, and their options (`format:`,
    `sheet:` and the rest) with them.

    The type of each column is guessed from its first 1,000 rows: Bool, Int, Float, Date (ISO 8601),
    DateTime or Str. A later value that does not fit stops the run naming the row, the column and the value
    (Z0606); nothing is ever coerced to `NA` silently. One later value is taken in: a decimal in a column
    guessed as whole numbers. The column is then read as Float, which keeps every whole number as it was,
    and a note names the first row that holds a decimal; `types: {col: Int}` keeps the column to whole
    numbers, and there the decimal stops the run. Empty cells, `NA` and `.` are missing, and `na_values:`
    adds codes; `Inf` and `-Inf` are infinity; `NaN` becomes `NA` with a note. Dates are read only in the
    ISO form, or with the format `types:` gives; a column that looks like dates in another form is read as
    text, with a note that proposes the `types:` entry. Variable names are kept as the file has them unless
    `names: snake` turns them into valid snake_case names. Every import prints one note saying what was read
    and what the format carries; further notes report what the reader changed or could not keep.

ARGUMENTS
    path        Str. The file, written with `/` from the project folder, or `root:file` for a data root. A
                path outside the project is refused (Z0701), and one that differs only in case from the
                file's real name is refused with the real spelling (Z0702).
    delim:      Str. The delimiter, one character. When omitted, `,` is used, or `;` or a tab when the first
                line has one of them and no comma.
    header:     Bool = true. Whether the first row names the columns. With `false` the columns are `v1`,
                `v2`, ….
    skip:       Int = 0. Lines to skip at the top of the file, before the header.
    na_values:  List. Values read as missing besides the empty cell, `NA` and `.`, as numbers or text:
                `na_values: [-99, -98]`.
    types:      Record. A record declaring a column's type when the guess is wrong: `types: {hours: Str}`,
                or a date format, `types: {sold: Date("%d/%m/%Y")}`. A column may be named as the file
                writes it or by the name `names: snake` gives it.
    names:      keep | snake = keep. Keep the names of the file, or turn each into a snake_case name and
                print the changes.
    quote:      Str. The quote character, one character; `"` by default. A quoted value may hold the
                delimiter and line breaks, and the quote itself written twice. `quote: ""` reads every
                character literally, for a file that never quotes.
    decimal:    Str. The decimal mark, one character; `.` by default. With `decimal: ","` the delimiter is
                `;` unless the first line shows a tab or `delim:` says otherwise, and `1,5` reads as a
                number while `1.5` is text.
    thousands:  Str. The character between groups of three digits, which a number may carry: `"."` in a
                German file, `","` in an English one with quoted values such as `"1,234"`, `" "` in a French
                one. Digits grouped any other way are text. It cannot be the decimal mark.
    encoding:   Str = "utf-8". The file's encoding when it is not UTF-8, by its name on the WHATWG list:
                `"windows-1252"` for files from Western European Windows programs, `"iso-8859-15"`,
                `"utf-16le"` for Unicode text exported by Excel, `"shift_jis"`. A byte the encoding cannot
                decode stops the import naming the row (Z0608).

VALUE
    A `Frame` with one column per variable of the file.

MISSING VALUES
    An empty cell, `NA` and `.` are read as missing in a column of any type. `na_values:` adds the codes a
    file uses for a missing value, such as `-99`, and a note counts the values each code turned into `NA`.
    `NaN` in a column of numbers becomes `NA` with a note. Nothing else becomes `NA`: a value that does not
    fit the type of its column stops the import and names the row (Z0606).

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    Note    the delimiter detected, a byte-order mark removed, blank lines skipped, names changed, values
            read as NA from na_values: or NaN, and a column that looks like dates read as text
    W0601   a text column holds values that differ only in Unicode normalisation form
    W0702   date-and-time values carried an offset and were converted to UTC
    Z0101   types: names a column the file does not have
    Z0401   delim:, skip:, na_values: or types: holds a value of the wrong kind
    Z0605   an empty, repeated or case-clashing column name, or a file without a header line
    Note    a column guessed as whole numbers from its first 1,000 rows read as Float because a later row
            holds a decimal
    Z0606   a value after the first 1,000 rows, or in a declared column, does not fit the column's type:
            text in a numeric column, a value that is not a date in a Date column, a decimal in a column
            declared Int
    Z0607   a row with the wrong number of fields, or a quote never closed
    Z0608   a row that is not UTF-8
    Z0701   a path outside the project and its data roots
    Z0702   a path that differs only in case from the file's name
    Z0715   no file at the path

A COMMON MISTAKE
    Misspelling the name of the file. Nothing is read in its place, and the message offers the nearest name:

    > import("zeff:wage.csv")
    error[Z0715]: no file `zeff:wage.csv` ships with Zeff
      = help: did you mean `zeff:wages.csv`?

    Write the name as it is: `import("zeff:wages.csv")`. A file of the project is written from the project
    folder, as in `data/wages.csv`; a place on the computer outside the project is refused (Z0701), so that
    a project runs the same on any machine.

COMING FROM R OR STATA
    Equivalents: R readr, readxl, haven, arrow, jsonlite, rio::import(); Stata import delimited, import
    excel, use.

    Zeff                                R                                                     Stata
    import("data/wages.csv")            read.csv("data/wages.csv")                            import delimited "data/wages.csv", clear
    import("data/wages.tsv")            read.delim("data/wages.tsv")                          import delimited "data/wages.tsv", clear
    import("f.csv", types: {id: Str})   read.csv("f.csv", colClasses = c(id = "character"))   import delimited "f.csv", stringcols(1) clear
    import("f.csv", na_values: [-99])   read.csv("f.csv", na.strings = c("", "NA", "-99"))    import delimited "f.csv", clear, then mvdecode _all, mv(-99)

    R's `read.csv` reads a column of numbers that holds one stray word as text, the whole column, and says
    nothing; Stata's `import delimited` makes it a string variable. Here the type guessed from the first
    1,000 rows is kept to, and a later value that does not fit stops the import and names the row, so a
    stray code is found on the day the file is read. `read.csv` starts from the working directory, which
    `setwd` moves; here a path always starts at the project folder, so a script reads the same file from
    wherever it is run. Stata replaces the dataset in memory, which is why it asks for `clear`; here the
    frame is a value, bound to a name with `let`.

RELATED TASKS
    - To write a frame to a file, use `save`.
    - For the datasets that ship with Zeff, with their labels and categories, use `dataset`.
    - To see the type and the missing values of each variable read, use `describe`.
    - To type a small frame into the script, use `frame`.
    - To change the type of a variable after reading, use `to`.

ACCURACY
    Exact.

SEE ALSO
    frame, describe, n_rows, variables

EXAMPLES
    // A file that ships with Zeff is read like any other; the note says what was read. The file carries the
    // group as a code, where `dataset(sleep)` carries its label.
    > let sleep = import("zeff:sleep.csv")
    > sleep.head(3)
    note: zeff:sleep.csv read as CSV: 20 rows × 3 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 1)
    frame · 3 rows × 3 variables

          extra   group   id
      1     0.7       1    1
      2    -1.6       1    2
      3    -0.2       1    3

    // What each variable was read as: a date written as ISO 8601 is a Date.
    > import("zeff:firms.csv").describe()
    frame · 400 rows × 4 variables

      variable   type   non-missing   missing   example
      firm       Int            400         0   1
      industry   Str            400         0   Agriculture
      size       Int            400         0   3
      founded    Date           400         0   1990-11-06
    note: zeff:firms.csv read as CSV: 400 rows × 4 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 3)

    // A type declared where the guess is not the one wanted: the group is a code, kept as text.
    > import("zeff:sleep.csv", types: {group: Str}).describe()
    frame · 20 rows × 3 variables

      variable   type    non-missing   missing   example
      extra      Float            20         0   0.7
      group      Str              20         0   1
      id         Int              20         0   1
    note: zeff:sleep.csv read as CSV: 20 rows × 3 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 4)

    // A code that a file uses for a missing value. Were 0 the code for a night not measured, the note would
    // count what it turned into `NA`.
    > import("zeff:sleep.csv", na_values: [0]).describe()
    frame · 20 rows × 3 variables

      variable   type    non-missing   missing   example
      extra      Float            19         1   0.7
      group      Int              20         0   1
      id         Int              20         0   1
    note: zeff:sleep.csv read as CSV: 20 rows × 3 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 5)
    note: 1 value of extra read as NA from na_values 0 (line 5)

    // A file whose first line is not a header: the columns are `v1`, `v2`, …. Here the header is skipped as
    // a line of its own.
    > import("zeff:sleep.csv", header: false, skip: 1).head(2)
    frame · 2 rows × 3 variables

            v1   v2   v3
      1    0.7    1    1
      2   -1.6    1    2
    note: zeff:sleep.csv read as CSV: 20 rows × 3 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 6)

    // The delimiter named. A wrong one shows at once: a single variable holding whole lines.
    > import("zeff:sleep.csv", delim: ";").head(2)
    frame · 2 rows × 1 variable

          extra,group,id
      1   0.7,1,1
      2   -1.6,1,2
    note: zeff:sleep.csv read as CSV: 20 rows × 1 variable, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 7)

    // `names: snake` turns each name into a snake_case one and reports the changes; these names are that
    // already, so nothing is reported. `quote: ""` reads a file that never quotes, every character as it
    // stands.
    > import("zeff:firms.csv", names: snake, quote: "").head(2)
    frame · 2 rows × 4 variables

          firm   industry      size   founded
      1      1   Agriculture      3   1990-11-06
      2      2   Agriculture      5   1957-07-02
    note: zeff:firms.csv read as CSV: 400 rows × 4 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 8)

    // The options for a file from a German spreadsheet: a decimal comma, which makes `;` the delimiter, a
    // point between groups of digits, and Windows-1252. The shipped file is not written so, and the result
    // says it at once.
    > import("zeff:sleep.csv", decimal: ",", thousands: ".", encoding: "windows-1252").head(2)
    frame · 2 rows × 1 variable

          extra,group,id
      1   0.7,1,1
      2   -1.6,1,2
    note: zeff:sleep.csv read as CSV: 20 rows × 1 variable, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 9)
